1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,300
November 12, 2026.
Santa Clara, California.

2
00:00:04,300 --> 00:00:09,898
In Santa Clara, Jensen Huang's thirty-three
years as Nvidia's boss just ended.

3
00:00:09,900 --> 00:00:15,428
At the center of the story: a woman U.S.
officials reportedly call a Chinese asset…

4
00:00:15,433 --> 00:00:18,033
She hasn't been charged with any crime.

5
00:00:18,033 --> 00:00:19,787
Did the wall work?

6
00:00:19,800 --> 00:00:22,067
Define… "work."

7
00:00:22,067 --> 00:00:24,567
That leaves two big questions.

8
00:00:24,567 --> 00:00:29,433
How did 7.5 billion dollars of
Nvidia chips end up in this story?

9
00:00:29,433 --> 00:00:32,941
And did Washington's
export wall actually leak?

10
00:00:32,967 --> 00:00:38,829
Because 240,000 of the world's most wanted
chips are sitting somewhere right now.

11
00:00:38,833 --> 00:00:41,233
It starts… with a diner booth.

12
00:00:41,233 --> 00:00:44,860
April 1993. San Jose, California.

13
00:00:44,867 --> 00:00:48,267
Jensen Huang co-founds
Nvidia in a Denny's booth.

14
00:00:48,267 --> 00:00:51,000
He runs the company
for thirty-three years.

15
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:54,900
Three engineers, one booth
— and bottomless coffee.

16
00:00:54,900 --> 00:00:56,592
Did you have a business plan?

17
00:00:56,600 --> 00:01:00,264
We had an idea. And… a LOT of refills.

18
00:01:00,267 --> 00:01:01,867
And the leather jacket?

19
00:01:01,867 --> 00:01:04,832
The jacket came later.
The coffee came first.

20
00:01:04,833 --> 00:01:07,433
So… I was born in a diner.

21
00:01:07,433 --> 00:01:09,882
The best ones usually are.

22
00:01:09,900 --> 00:01:11,200
His original goal?

23
00:01:11,200 --> 00:01:14,616
Make video games look
less like… wet cardboard.

24
00:01:14,633 --> 00:01:18,233
Standard computer chips work
like one genius professor.

25
00:01:18,233 --> 00:01:19,264
Brilliant.

26
00:01:19,267 --> 00:01:20,664
Very serious.

27
00:01:20,667 --> 00:01:23,833
But he solves just ONE
math problem at a time.

28
00:01:23,833 --> 00:01:25,890
Nvidia's chip works differently.

29
00:01:25,900 --> 00:01:29,033
Picture ten thousand
kindergarteners holding crayons.

30
00:01:29,033 --> 00:01:30,792
None of them knows calculus…

31
00:01:30,800 --> 00:01:32,246
Some eat the paste.

32
00:01:32,267 --> 00:01:35,367
But they ALL color at
the exact same moment.

33
00:01:35,367 --> 00:01:38,162
In tech speak, that's a GPU.

34
00:01:38,167 --> 00:01:41,433
A factory for tiny math, done all at once.

35
00:01:41,433 --> 00:01:44,315
Then AI researchers notice a secret.

36
00:01:44,333 --> 00:01:45,967
What is AI training?

37
00:01:45,967 --> 00:01:48,467
TRILLIONS of tiny math problems.

38
00:01:48,467 --> 00:01:51,048
Exactly what those
kindergarteners do best.

39
00:01:51,067 --> 00:01:53,767
I used to draw dragons for video games.

40
00:01:53,767 --> 00:01:56,061
Now you do math. Keep coloring.

41
00:01:56,067 --> 00:01:57,833
How many problems are left?

42
00:01:57,833 --> 00:01:59,840
A few trillion… give or take.

43
00:01:59,867 --> 00:02:01,800
My crayons are melting!

44
00:02:01,800 --> 00:02:04,733
That means it's working. Welcome to AI.

45
00:02:04,733 --> 00:02:09,813
Suddenly, every tech giant needs
Nvidia's flagship chips, like the H100.

46
00:02:09,833 --> 00:02:13,033
Each chip costs tens of
THOUSANDS of dollars.

47
00:02:13,033 --> 00:02:14,487
Does it come with a free game?

48
00:02:14,500 --> 00:02:15,821
Not a chance.

49
00:02:15,833 --> 00:02:17,463
How much is one of these chips?

50
00:02:17,467 --> 00:02:19,690
Tens of thousands of dollars… Each.

51
00:02:19,700 --> 00:02:23,100
Great. I'll take ten thousand of them!

52
00:02:23,100 --> 00:02:26,000
So will everyone else… There's a waitlist.

53
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:27,885
How LONG is the waitlist?!

54
00:02:27,900 --> 00:02:30,900
Well… how long is your patience?

55
00:02:30,900 --> 00:02:34,210
Nvidia isn't just selling
shovels in a gold rush…

56
00:02:34,233 --> 00:02:36,933
It's selling the ONLY shovels on earth.

57
00:02:36,933 --> 00:02:39,233
Why can't buyers just switch brands?

58
00:02:39,233 --> 00:02:43,263
Because AI engineers write their
code for Nvidia's own tools.

59
00:02:43,267 --> 00:02:45,667
And Beijing needs those shovels too.

60
00:02:45,667 --> 00:02:48,558
Why does China care so
much about a gaming chip?

61
00:02:48,567 --> 00:02:52,586
Because whoever owns the fastest
chips trains the smartest AI.

62
00:02:52,600 --> 00:02:57,133
And Washington worries smart AI
can also guide… modern weapons.

63
00:02:57,133 --> 00:02:59,062
That chip is a missile brain!

64
00:02:59,067 --> 00:03:01,667
It's a very fast… calculator.

65
00:03:01,667 --> 00:03:03,944
A calculator that does missile math!

66
00:03:03,967 --> 00:03:06,167
Mostly, it makes cat videos.

67
00:03:06,167 --> 00:03:07,912
And what do the cats want?

68
00:03:07,933 --> 00:03:09,500
…I'm going to need a minute.

69
00:03:09,500 --> 00:03:12,078
Caught in the middle? Jensen himself.

70
00:03:12,100 --> 00:03:14,800
What does the man with
the keys actually think?

71
00:03:14,800 --> 00:03:17,333
He thinks the wall… backfires.

72
00:03:17,333 --> 00:03:21,403
May 21, 2025. Computex, Taipei.

73
00:03:21,433 --> 00:03:25,667
"All in all, the export control
was a failure." Jensen Huang.

74
00:03:25,667 --> 00:03:30,428
Before the rules, Nvidia held ninety-five
percent of China's AI chip market.

75
00:03:30,433 --> 00:03:32,021
By May 2025?

76
00:03:32,033 --> 00:03:33,695
Down to fifty percent.

77
00:03:33,700 --> 00:03:36,500
If you block our chips,
China builds its own.

78
00:03:36,500 --> 00:03:38,200
Then we'll block those too!

79
00:03:38,200 --> 00:03:41,961
Chips made in China? By China? For China?

80
00:03:41,967 --> 00:03:43,800
We'll… find a way.

81
00:03:43,800 --> 00:03:45,280
With what, exactly?

82
00:03:45,300 --> 00:03:46,700
More paperwork!

83
00:03:46,700 --> 00:03:48,552
Washington hears that warning…

84
00:03:48,567 --> 00:03:50,300
And bolts the door anyway.

85
00:03:50,300 --> 00:03:54,225
October 7, 2022. Washington, D.C.

86
00:03:54,233 --> 00:03:57,167
Washington builds a wall
across the Pacific…

87
00:03:57,167 --> 00:03:59,300
It's a wall made of paperwork.

88
00:03:59,300 --> 00:04:04,880
In October 2022, export controls block
top AI chips from going to China.

89
00:04:04,900 --> 00:04:07,233
The rule works like a highway speed limit.

90
00:04:07,233 --> 00:04:08,500
Is your chip too fast?

91
00:04:08,500 --> 00:04:10,200
You need a federal license.

92
00:04:10,200 --> 00:04:11,467
Shipping to Beijing?

93
00:04:11,467 --> 00:04:13,311
Good luck getting that stamp.

94
00:04:13,333 --> 00:04:15,200
So how does Nvidia respond?

95
00:04:15,200 --> 00:04:19,267
Engineers design slightly
slower chips — just for China.

96
00:04:19,267 --> 00:04:20,367
Problem solved?

97
00:04:20,367 --> 00:04:21,533
Not for long.

98
00:04:21,533 --> 00:04:25,614
In October 2023, Washington
lowers the speed limit again.

99
00:04:25,633 --> 00:04:28,067
Those China-only chips get banned too.

100
00:04:28,067 --> 00:04:32,332
So Nvidia designs an even
SLOWER chip: the H20.

101
00:04:32,333 --> 00:04:35,319
Think of a Ferrari… with
a lawnmower engine.

102
00:04:35,333 --> 00:04:37,000
Why am I so slow?

103
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:38,933
You're the LEGAL model.

104
00:04:38,933 --> 00:04:43,700
Then, in April 2025, Washington
restricts the H20 as well.

105
00:04:43,700 --> 00:04:48,400
Nvidia takes a 5.5-billion-dollar
charge on unsold inventory.

106
00:04:48,400 --> 00:04:51,557
They baked the cake, and
Uncle Sam cancelled the party.

107
00:04:51,567 --> 00:04:53,733
This chip is too fast for China.

108
00:04:53,733 --> 00:04:56,044
No problem! We made a slower one.

109
00:04:56,067 --> 00:04:58,067
That one is also too fast.

110
00:04:58,067 --> 00:05:00,428
So we made an even slower one!

111
00:05:00,433 --> 00:05:02,333
Still… too fast.

112
00:05:02,333 --> 00:05:04,770
What if I just ship the empty box?

113
00:05:04,800 --> 00:05:06,233
Fill out form seven.

114
00:05:06,233 --> 00:05:09,433
Every physical door to
China looks locked tight.

115
00:05:09,433 --> 00:05:10,100
Why?

116
00:05:10,100 --> 00:05:13,148
Because the rules watch
where physical boxes go…

117
00:05:13,167 --> 00:05:15,100
Not where the math goes.

118
00:05:15,100 --> 00:05:17,690
2024. Southeast Asia.

119
00:05:17,700 --> 00:05:21,267
Border guards are GREAT at
stopping cardboard boxes.

120
00:05:21,267 --> 00:05:24,080
They are terrible at stopping Wi-Fi.

121
00:05:24,100 --> 00:05:27,367
Imagine your mom bans a
PlayStation from your bedroom.

122
00:05:27,367 --> 00:05:28,767
How do you beat the ban?

123
00:05:28,767 --> 00:05:31,629
Your neighbor buys ten
PlayStations instead.

124
00:05:31,633 --> 00:05:34,658
He keeps every console
inside his OWN house.

125
00:05:34,667 --> 00:05:37,832
Then he runs a long controller
cable through your window.

126
00:05:37,833 --> 00:05:39,367
You don't move the machine…

127
00:05:39,367 --> 00:05:41,040
You just move the game.

128
00:05:41,067 --> 00:05:44,064
In Silicon Valley, that's
called cloud computing.

129
00:05:44,067 --> 00:05:48,367
Shipping an H100 chip to
China requires a U.S. license.

130
00:05:48,367 --> 00:05:50,500
Putting it in a data center in Malaysia?

131
00:05:50,500 --> 00:05:52,152
Generally… legal.

132
00:05:52,167 --> 00:05:55,267
Letting overseas clients rent
that power over the internet?

133
00:05:55,267 --> 00:05:56,488
Also legal.

134
00:05:56,500 --> 00:05:58,767
Did any of these chips go to China?

135
00:05:58,767 --> 00:06:02,106
Nope. The servers stay
riiight here in Malaysia.

136
00:06:02,133 --> 00:06:03,967
Then what leaves the building?

137
00:06:03,967 --> 00:06:06,396
Just the login… and the answers.

138
00:06:06,400 --> 00:06:08,424
Is there a form for answers?

139
00:06:08,433 --> 00:06:09,767
You tell me, man.

140
00:06:09,767 --> 00:06:11,500
…Fill out form seven.

141
00:06:11,500 --> 00:06:13,800
Lawmakers have a name for this gap.

142
00:06:13,800 --> 00:06:15,395
The cloud loophole.

143
00:06:15,400 --> 00:06:16,791
Remember the neighbor…

144
00:06:16,800 --> 00:06:21,100
Because in Singapore, a tiny firm
is about to test that cable.

145
00:06:21,100 --> 00:06:23,767
March 2023. Singapore.

146
00:06:23,767 --> 00:06:28,167
In March 2023, a small
Singapore firm changes its name.

147
00:06:28,167 --> 00:06:29,118
The new name?

148
00:06:29,133 --> 00:06:30,354
Megaspeed.

149
00:06:30,367 --> 00:06:34,267
Which sounds less like a cloud company
and more like a roller coaster.

150
00:06:34,267 --> 00:06:38,764
Its director is a businesswoman
named Huang Le, known as Alice Huang.

151
00:06:38,767 --> 00:06:41,227
Same last name as Nvidia's CEO.

152
00:06:41,233 --> 00:06:43,033
Zero family relation.

153
00:06:43,033 --> 00:06:44,989
What is Megaspeed's business model?

154
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:49,225
Buy Nvidia servers, plug them in,
and rent out the computing power.

155
00:06:49,233 --> 00:06:52,495
Megaspeed becomes an official
Nvidia cloud partner.

156
00:06:52,500 --> 00:06:56,467
Think of an authorized car
dealership, but for AI supercomputers.

157
00:06:56,467 --> 00:06:57,895
Everyone wins…

158
00:06:57,900 --> 00:07:00,100
Especially Nvidia's accountants.

159
00:07:00,100 --> 00:07:04,260
Next, Megaspeed sets up a
Malaysian arm called Speedmatrix.

160
00:07:04,267 --> 00:07:05,300
Why Malaysia?

161
00:07:05,300 --> 00:07:09,467
Cheap land, big power grids —
and a short cable from Singapore.

162
00:07:09,467 --> 00:07:12,382
Delivery for Speedmatrix!
Where do these plug in?

163
00:07:12,400 --> 00:07:14,267
Right across the bridge from Singapore.

164
00:07:14,267 --> 00:07:16,595
And how many servers are we talking about?

165
00:07:16,600 --> 00:07:17,500
Yes.

166
00:07:17,500 --> 00:07:19,300
That's— that's not a number.

167
00:07:19,300 --> 00:07:21,273
It will be… Keep unloading.

168
00:07:21,300 --> 00:07:24,033
Then this tiny company goes shopping.

169
00:07:24,033 --> 00:07:26,524
Reuters checked Megaspeed's
corporate filings.

170
00:07:26,533 --> 00:07:31,133
In 2023, Megaspeed held about
33 million dollars in assets.

171
00:07:31,133 --> 00:07:32,500
By 2024?

172
00:07:32,500 --> 00:07:34,075
Three BILLION dollars.

173
00:07:34,100 --> 00:07:39,567
Revenue jumped from ZERO to 294
million dollars in one year.

174
00:07:39,567 --> 00:07:43,799
That's like opening a lemonade stand
and owning the orchard by Christmas.

175
00:07:43,800 --> 00:07:47,133
Wait. This tiny company
gets Nvidia's best chips?

176
00:07:47,133 --> 00:07:49,300
Easily… according to the pitch.

177
00:07:49,300 --> 00:07:51,520
Everyone else has been waiting for months!

178
00:07:51,533 --> 00:07:54,100
Does that seem suspicious to you?

179
00:07:54,100 --> 00:07:56,233
It seems like a great investment to me!

180
00:07:56,233 --> 00:07:59,493
Those are… sometimes the same thing.

181
00:07:59,500 --> 00:08:04,467
According to the Financial Times,
Alice Huang pitched investors in 2025.

182
00:08:04,467 --> 00:08:08,833
An investor told the paper she said
getting Nvidia chips was NO problem.

183
00:08:08,833 --> 00:08:13,227
And she reportedly named Chinese
tech giant Alibaba as a client.

184
00:08:13,233 --> 00:08:19,583
The Financial Times also reports an Alibaba
unit lent money to Speedmatrix in June 2024.

185
00:08:19,600 --> 00:08:26,300
Meanwhile, cameras catch Alice Huang near
Jensen at Taipei events in 2024 and 2025.

186
00:08:26,300 --> 00:08:31,200
Nobody recorded what they said. Here's
roughly how that small talk goes.

187
00:08:31,200 --> 00:08:34,167
I don't think we've met.
And I meet everybody.

188
00:08:34,167 --> 00:08:37,891
Alice. Megaspeed. We're a
cloud company in Singapore.

189
00:08:37,900 --> 00:08:41,000
Cloud! I love cloud. How big are you?

190
00:08:41,000 --> 00:08:44,567
Oh — small. For now.

191
00:08:44,567 --> 00:08:47,823
Everybody says "for now."
How many chips do you need?

192
00:08:47,833 --> 00:08:49,216
How many do you have?

193
00:08:49,233 --> 00:08:52,267
I LIKE her. Somebody get her a chair!

194
00:08:52,267 --> 00:08:56,300
Nvidia says meeting a big
customer is normal for a CEO.

195
00:08:56,300 --> 00:08:59,767
Look at this. Same party… same table.

196
00:08:59,767 --> 00:09:02,815
It's a trade show. Everyone's
at the same table.

197
00:09:02,833 --> 00:09:04,933
She's standing RIGHT next to him.

198
00:09:04,933 --> 00:09:07,233
So is… the shrimp buffet.

199
00:09:07,233 --> 00:09:08,514
So it's nothing?

200
00:09:08,533 --> 00:09:10,733
It's networking. Write that down.

201
00:09:10,733 --> 00:09:15,507
Then, in February 2025…
someone in authority notices.

202
00:09:15,533 --> 00:09:20,900
Singapore police and customs open an investigation
into Megaspeed's server declarations.

203
00:09:20,900 --> 00:09:25,533
Police hold Alice Huang's passport to keep
her around, since she doesn't live there.

204
00:09:25,533 --> 00:09:26,667
Yet in public?

205
00:09:26,667 --> 00:09:28,445
Nothing visible happens.

206
00:09:28,467 --> 00:09:30,627
The shipments keep rolling in.

207
00:09:30,633 --> 00:09:34,485
2024 to 2026. Johor, Malaysia.

208
00:09:34,500 --> 00:09:37,467
Just how many chips flowed
into this pipeline?

209
00:09:37,467 --> 00:09:39,533
Three newsrooms checked the records.

210
00:09:39,533 --> 00:09:41,207
Three counts came back.

211
00:09:41,233 --> 00:09:44,567
Count one: Bloomberg checked
Malaysian customs records.

212
00:09:44,567 --> 00:09:51,467
It found at least 4.6 billion dollars
of hardware, or 136,000 Nvidia chips.

213
00:09:51,467 --> 00:09:53,333
That's a LOT of chips!

214
00:09:53,333 --> 00:09:55,567
That was… count one.

215
00:09:55,567 --> 00:10:01,463
Count two: The New York Times checked
trade data from mid-2024 to early 2026.

216
00:10:01,467 --> 00:10:05,729
It counted at least 4.8
billion dollars of Nvidia gear.

217
00:10:05,733 --> 00:10:09,961
Count three: The Information cited
two people familiar with the deals.

218
00:10:09,967 --> 00:10:16,133
Their total hit 7.5 billion
dollars—roughly 240,000 chips.

219
00:10:16,133 --> 00:10:18,355
Different records. Different totals.

220
00:10:18,367 --> 00:10:20,032
Let's shrink those numbers down.

221
00:10:20,033 --> 00:10:24,155
The biggest count works out to
roughly 31,000 dollars per chip…

222
00:10:24,167 --> 00:10:27,133
That's a nice used car. For each chip.

223
00:10:27,133 --> 00:10:29,667
The Times' count covers
about twenty months.

224
00:10:29,667 --> 00:10:34,557
That's more than 300,000 dollars
of Nvidia gear every SINGLE hour.

225
00:10:34,567 --> 00:10:39,327
More than THREE Las Vegas Spheres,
or almost half the Dallas Cowboys.

226
00:10:39,333 --> 00:10:41,300
Three hundred thousand an hour?

227
00:10:41,300 --> 00:10:42,933
Every. Hour.

228
00:10:42,933 --> 00:10:44,190
Even on Sundays?

229
00:10:44,200 --> 00:10:46,163
Especially Sundays.

230
00:10:46,167 --> 00:10:48,933
Bloomberg also noticed a strange gap…

231
00:10:48,933 --> 00:10:53,400
Megaspeed's declared data-center space
looked too small for that much hardware.

232
00:10:53,400 --> 00:10:56,727
Picture buying a THOUSAND
couches for a studio apartment.

233
00:10:56,733 --> 00:10:58,585
Are we training AI yet?

234
00:10:58,600 --> 00:11:00,022
Shh! Not yet.

235
00:11:00,033 --> 00:11:01,800
Then why is it so dark?

236
00:11:01,800 --> 00:11:04,200
Because… we're still in the box.

237
00:11:04,200 --> 00:11:05,750
When do we get plugged in?

238
00:11:05,767 --> 00:11:07,733
When they find a bigger apartment.

239
00:11:07,733 --> 00:11:10,498
But here is the part most headlines skip.

240
00:11:10,500 --> 00:11:13,563
Bloomberg found NO evidence
the chips went to China.

241
00:11:13,567 --> 00:11:18,233
And Megaspeed says it runs a "small
commercial cloud," as U.S. rules allow.

242
00:11:18,233 --> 00:11:20,859
So why did U.S. intelligence step in?

243
00:11:20,867 --> 00:11:24,286
Spring 2026. Washington, D.C.

244
00:11:24,300 --> 00:11:30,467
On February 26, 2026, Singapore police
hand Alice Huang her passport back.

245
00:11:30,467 --> 00:11:32,940
She leaves Singapore the SAME day.

246
00:11:32,967 --> 00:11:37,533
Weeks later, in Washington…
a classified memo circulates.

247
00:11:37,533 --> 00:11:42,133
Everything known about this memo comes from
anonymous sources speaking to reporters.

248
00:11:42,133 --> 00:11:46,900
According to The New York Times,
Secretary of State Marco Rubio oversaw it.

249
00:11:46,900 --> 00:11:50,867
The Times reports it drew on electronic
surveillance of Alice Huang.

250
00:11:50,867 --> 00:11:54,533
In plain English: someone
was reportedly listening.

251
00:11:54,533 --> 00:11:56,819
This could be… big.

252
00:11:56,833 --> 00:11:57,795
How big?

253
00:11:57,800 --> 00:11:59,400
Write-a-memo big.

254
00:11:59,400 --> 00:12:01,421
Ugh… I hate memos.

255
00:12:01,433 --> 00:12:03,300
Should we tell somebody first?

256
00:12:03,300 --> 00:12:05,267
First, we schedule a meeting.

257
00:12:05,267 --> 00:12:06,752
A meeting about what?

258
00:12:06,767 --> 00:12:08,533
About… the meeting.

259
00:12:08,533 --> 00:12:10,753
What did the memo allegedly say?

260
00:12:10,767 --> 00:12:12,588
According to The New York Times,

261
00:12:12,588 --> 00:12:16,000
it claimed China's Ministry of
State Security recruited her.

262
00:12:16,000 --> 00:12:18,363
That's China's top spy agency.

263
00:12:18,367 --> 00:12:23,333
Think CIA and FBI in one building,
with fewer press releases.

264
00:12:23,333 --> 00:12:28,072
According to the Times, U.S. officials
believe the agency had two goals for her.

265
00:12:28,100 --> 00:12:32,400
Goal one: learn about America's
most advanced AI chips.

266
00:12:32,400 --> 00:12:36,493
Goal two: push Nvidia toward
selling those chips to China.

267
00:12:36,500 --> 00:12:40,595
And the Financial Times reports
the memo alleged a personal link.

268
00:12:40,600 --> 00:12:45,100
It reportedly alleged a romance
between Alice Huang and Jensen Huang.

269
00:12:45,100 --> 00:12:46,987
Now… pause right here.

270
00:12:47,000 --> 00:12:49,500
Lawyers, this is your favorite part.

271
00:12:49,500 --> 00:12:52,233
What do the same reports
say in the fine print?

272
00:12:52,233 --> 00:12:57,450
According to the Times, U.S. officials
DON'T believe Jensen knew about any spying.

273
00:12:57,467 --> 00:13:00,396
Beijing rejects the spy claims as false.

274
00:13:00,400 --> 00:13:04,500
Singapore states it never investigated
Alice Huang for espionage.

275
00:13:04,500 --> 00:13:08,133
Singapore police say they found
insufficient evidence to charge her.

276
00:13:08,133 --> 00:13:11,609
Though Singapore DID bar her
from re-entering the country.

277
00:13:11,633 --> 00:13:15,533
Police there say she knowingly
downplayed her role at Megaspeed.

278
00:13:15,533 --> 00:13:19,494
Still, no country has charged
Alice Huang with any crime.

279
00:13:19,500 --> 00:13:21,157
Not one charge.

280
00:13:21,167 --> 00:13:23,167
This is a spy operation!

281
00:13:23,167 --> 00:13:26,285
It's a leaked memo… that nobody has seen.

282
00:13:26,300 --> 00:13:27,933
There were wiretaps!

283
00:13:27,933 --> 00:13:31,385
Reported wiretaps. And
how many indictments?

284
00:13:31,400 --> 00:13:33,033
…None. So far.

285
00:13:33,033 --> 00:13:35,257
Call me when "so far" is over.

286
00:13:35,267 --> 00:13:39,451
Why does an unverified memo in
Washington matter to your bank account?

287
00:13:39,467 --> 00:13:43,433
Because Nvidia is about
six percent of the S&P 500.

288
00:13:43,433 --> 00:13:46,384
Got 10,000 dollars in an S&P 500 fund?

289
00:13:46,400 --> 00:13:49,059
About 600 dollars of it is Nvidia.

290
00:13:49,067 --> 00:13:50,567
Nobody is charged?

291
00:13:50,567 --> 00:13:51,433
Nobody.

292
00:13:51,433 --> 00:13:53,400
Tell that to my 401(k)!

293
00:13:53,400 --> 00:13:57,584
And by late October, the allegations
are already in the newspapers.

294
00:13:57,600 --> 00:14:02,267
November 5 to 8, 2026.
Santa Clara, California.

295
00:14:02,267 --> 00:14:07,567
On November 5, 2026, three House
committee chairs send Jensen a letter.

296
00:14:07,567 --> 00:14:12,325
They request a briefing on a "publicly
reported Chinese intelligence asset."

297
00:14:12,333 --> 00:14:13,932
Notice two words…

298
00:14:13,933 --> 00:14:15,700
"Publicly reported."

299
00:14:15,700 --> 00:14:18,233
Even Congress is citing the newspaper.

300
00:14:18,233 --> 00:14:21,882
On November 8, the Times
reports on the classified memo.

301
00:14:21,900 --> 00:14:26,126
The same day, Jensen sends a
memo to every Nvidia employee.

302
00:14:26,133 --> 00:14:27,667
What does his memo say?

303
00:14:27,667 --> 00:14:29,728
"I was unfaithful to my wife."

304
00:14:29,733 --> 00:14:35,408
Nvidia's board opens an independent investigation
into whether foreign intelligence targeted him.

305
00:14:35,433 --> 00:14:37,600
And what about Nvidia's technology?

306
00:14:37,600 --> 00:14:41,900
The board says it sees "no reason to
believe" secrets were compromised.

307
00:14:41,900 --> 00:14:45,488
Nvidia also says it stopped
doing business with Megaspeed.

308
00:14:45,500 --> 00:14:49,533
The next trading day, Nvidia's
stock drops about four percent.

309
00:14:49,533 --> 00:14:50,833
Is that bad?

310
00:14:50,833 --> 00:14:52,948
Ask me… Thursday.

311
00:14:52,967 --> 00:14:55,833
Four days later, the chair goes empty.

312
00:14:55,833 --> 00:15:00,167
November 12, 2026. Capitol
Hill and Santa Clara.

313
00:15:00,167 --> 00:15:05,112
On November 12, 2026, Jensen
Huang resigns as CEO.

314
00:15:05,133 --> 00:15:08,533
Finance chief Colette
Kress becomes interim CEO.

315
00:15:08,533 --> 00:15:12,167
Nvidia's stock falls about
NINE percent in early trading.

316
00:15:12,167 --> 00:15:14,233
What did that drop do to your money?

317
00:15:14,233 --> 00:15:18,488
Remember the 600-dollar Nvidia
slice in your 10,000-dollar fund?

318
00:15:18,500 --> 00:15:22,652
A nine percent drop shaves off
about fifty dollars before lunch.

319
00:15:22,667 --> 00:15:27,367
Even if you never picked a stock…
the chip war reaches your retirement.

320
00:15:27,367 --> 00:15:31,367
Today, four sides see
four DIFFERENT realities.

321
00:15:31,367 --> 00:15:36,233
Washington hawks say Megaspeed proves
the export wall needs cloud rules.

322
00:15:36,233 --> 00:15:41,702
Nvidia's camp says cutting off buyers only
forces China to build rival chips faster.

323
00:15:41,733 --> 00:15:46,367
Megaspeed insists it ran a lawful
commercial cloud under U.S. rules.

324
00:15:46,367 --> 00:15:49,867
And Beijing rejects the
espionage story as false.

325
00:15:49,867 --> 00:15:52,151
Ban the cloud rentals. All of them!

326
00:15:52,167 --> 00:15:55,100
Then China builds its
own chips even faster.

327
00:15:55,100 --> 00:15:57,100
Then we write faster rules!

328
00:15:57,100 --> 00:15:59,098
How fast can you write a rule?

329
00:15:59,100 --> 00:16:01,220
Eighteen months! If we hurry.

330
00:16:01,233 --> 00:16:03,998
Wonderful… Very fast paperwork.

331
00:16:04,000 --> 00:16:06,633
Remember the neighbor
with ten PlayStations?

332
00:16:06,633 --> 00:16:13,529
In January 2026, the House passed the
Remote Access Security Act, 369 to 22.

333
00:16:13,533 --> 00:16:16,667
It requires a license before
renting out the controller.

334
00:16:16,667 --> 00:16:21,377
So how did 7.5 billion dollars
of chips end up in this story?

335
00:16:21,400 --> 00:16:25,866
As far as anyone has shown… they DIDN'T
sneak into China in dark trucks.

336
00:16:25,867 --> 00:16:29,833
They went to Southeast Asia, where
renting them out was generally allowed.

337
00:16:29,833 --> 00:16:31,733
So did the wall leak?

338
00:16:31,733 --> 00:16:34,922
Maybe… but not the way you picture it.

339
00:16:34,933 --> 00:16:38,899
Reports say Chinese companies rented
that computing power from afar.

340
00:16:38,900 --> 00:16:42,267
Bloomberg found no proof the
chips themselves crossed over.

341
00:16:42,267 --> 00:16:45,233
The wall held. The
cable just went over it.

342
00:16:45,233 --> 00:16:49,758
America built a steel vault door…
in the middle of an open field.

343
00:16:49,767 --> 00:16:52,927
So… the CEO is gone.

344
00:16:52,933 --> 00:16:55,333
And where are the two
hundred forty thousand chips?

345
00:16:55,333 --> 00:16:57,400
Still humming away overseas.

346
00:16:57,400 --> 00:16:59,767
So did the wall work, or not?

347
00:16:59,767 --> 00:17:01,365
Define… "work."

348
00:17:01,367 --> 00:17:02,826
…Fill out form seven.

349
00:17:02,833 --> 00:17:08,583
One huge question remains unanswered: who
actually logged into all those servers?

350
00:17:08,600 --> 00:17:10,533
So here's what to watch next.

351
00:17:10,533 --> 00:17:16,031
November 17: Nvidia reports earnings,
its first without Jensen as CEO.

352
00:17:16,033 --> 00:17:20,554
November 18: the date three House
chairs asked Jensen to brief them.

353
00:17:20,567 --> 00:17:24,030
As of this video… nobody has
said if that still happens.

354
00:17:24,033 --> 00:17:27,500
The Remote Access Security Act
STILL hasn't passed the Senate.

355
00:17:27,500 --> 00:17:31,733
And Singapore's probe into Megaspeed
and six people is still open.

356
00:17:31,733 --> 00:17:34,333
In Santa Clara… the chair sits empty.

357
00:17:34,333 --> 00:17:40,141
And somewhere across the ocean, 240,000
green chips keep blinking in the dark.

